{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for significant upward bias in the first 5-10 days of the forecast horizon; the model consistently underestimates immediate price spikes (e.g., the jump to 76+ in Examples 1-5) and fails to capture the initial volatility magnitude.",
    "Apply a mean-reversion correction after sentiment spikes (values of 1.00 or 0.50); the model exhibits excessive inertia, predicting a slow, linear drift rather than the sharp pullback observed in true outcomes following high sentiment or price peaks.",
    "Reduce forecast values by 3-5 EUR in the mid-to-late horizon (days 15-30) when the model projects a sustained upward trend, as the true market data shows a distinct downward drift or stabilization at lower levels (around 68-70 EUR) after the initial spike.",
    "Adjust for lag in trend reversal: when history shows a peak followed by a drop, the model continues to forecast increases for 3-5 days before correcting; shift the forecast peak earlier by 2-3 days to align with actual market behavior.",
    "Ignore positive sentiment signals (1.00) if they are isolated and not supported by a sustained upward momentum in the 18-day history; the model overreacts to single-day positive sentiment by projecting a linear rally that does not materialize."
  ]
}
